// Many languages have compact conditional value branching in the form of ternary operators.
// In C/C++ and many others, it is like:   `cond ? x : y`
// Where <cond> is evaluated as a boolean, and returns <x> if true, and <y> if false.

// I do like the compactness of this, but I haven't really been a fan of it otherwise.
// And for Asa, it really wouldn't fit in. Some languages also re-use the `if` keyword, like:
//      `x if cond else y`
// But with the condition in the middle, I personally think it reads poorly

// Using the already builtin language features, you could just write it as an if-else with results:
if(cond) result x; else result y;
// But this is rather wordy compared to:
cond ? x : y

// This one saves space, but I honestly hate it:
x if(cond) else y


// TODO: Figure out ternary operator
// For now, I am going to not do anything. You can just use the existing if syntax:
printl(if(cond) result x; else result y;);


// I could add a new keyword, `when`. Then it wouldnt be stuck using `result`
// Like:
when(cond) x else y

// Basically, it is very similar to an if statement, but it implicitly `result`s 
// the entire scope body.
// For example, you could write the following with `if`:
if(cond){
    result x;
}
else{
    result y;
}

// Or the alternative using when:
when(cond){
    x
}
else{
    y
}

// Although `when` and `if` are practically synonyms, and `when` doesnt imply this functionality
// of implicitly resulting at all.
